// REJECTS: Unexpected token
//
// A MongoDB class mapping with `~mainCollection` written BEFORE `~filter`. The three commands
// are a fixed SEQUENCE in the rule, not a set: filter, then mainCollection, then binding, in
// that order and no other. The body below contains exactly the same three clauses as the one
// in ../fixtures/t2-mongodb.pure with the first two transposed.
//
// Ordering constraints are the cheapest thing for a rewrite to lose: a loop over "whichever
// clause comes next" is simpler to write than a sequence, handles the legal corpus perfectly,
// and is silently more permissive. Nothing downstream cares -- the protocol object has three
// independent slots -- so the divergence is invisible until two parsers disagree about whether
// a file is legal.
//
// legend-engine has this same shape in several grammars; ./ordering-constraint-clauses.pure
// and ./ordering-view-modifiers.pure pin two others. This is the MongoDB one.
###Mapping
Mapping fixture::neg::MongoMappingOutOfOrder
(
  *fixture::neg::Person[person_id]: MongoDB
  {
    ~mainCollection [fixture::neg::PersonDb] Person
    ~filter [fixture::neg::PersonDb] activeOnly
    ~binding fixture::neg::PersonBinding
  }
)
